lesson:
The browser turns your HTML into a tree of objects called the **DOM**, and JavaScript can read and change it. That's how pages react.

```
<p id="count">0</p>
<button id="add">Add one</button>
<script src="script.js"></script>
```

```
const count = document.getElementById("count");
const add = document.getElementById("add");
let n = 0;
add.addEventListener("click", () => {
  n++;
  count.textContent = n;
});
```

- `document.getElementById("x")` finds the element with `id="x"`; `document.querySelector(".card")` finds the first match for any CSS selector.
- `.textContent` reads or sets an element's text (safe: it never runs anything someone typed). `.value` reads what's typed in an input. `.style.color = "red"` and `.classList.add("done")` change looks.
- `addEventListener("click", function)` runs the function every time it happens. Other events: `"input"` (each keystroke in a box), `"change"`, `"submit"`.
- Put the `<script>` at the end of the body (or use `defer`), so the elements exist when the code runs.

Keep the logic (the maths, the rules) in plain functions, and the DOM code thin: plain functions can be tested on their own, which is exactly how these exercises are graded.

